CLR (HIP runtime): clamp the HIP-graph-replay flat-buffer dispatch's chunk
size to the actual hardware queue's capacity, instead of trusting
DEBUG_HIP_GRAPH_BATCH_SIZE's default (256) unconditionally.

WHY
---
vLLM (and any workload relying on `hipGraphLaunch`/CUDA-graph replay) hung
indefinitely on gfx803 -- `replay()` spun forever in
`VirtualGPU::WaitForQueueSlot`. Root-caused via real-hardware MQD register
inspection (`/sys/kernel/debug/kfd/mqds`, cross-checked against
`/proc/<pid>/mem` ring contents) down to `rocvirtual.cpp`'s
`dispatchAqlPacketBatchFlat` -- the fast-path batched dispatch HIP graph
replay uses to submit an entire captured graph with a single wptr bump
instead of one AQL enqueue per node.

That function submits packets in `kPeriod`-sized chunks
(`kPeriod = DEBUG_HIP_GRAPH_BATCH_SIZE`, a flag defaulting to 256), so that
graphs larger than the ring can still be replayed: the GPU drains earlier
chunks while the CPU writes later ones. This only works if a single chunk
never needs more in-flight ring capacity than the ring actually has.
`WaitForQueueSlot(write_index, capacity)`'s condition is
`(write_index - cached_read) < capacity` -- resolving it requires the CP to
have processed packets up to `write_index - capacity`. For a chunk's own
`WaitForQueueSlot` call, those are packets from THIS SAME CHUNK, which have
not been written or doorbell-rung yet (that happens after the wait
succeeds). If the chunk size ever exceeds the ring's usable capacity
(`sw_queue_size`, i.e. `queueMask`), the wait can never resolve by
construction -- no amount of draining by already-committed earlier work can
free room that only this chunk's own unwritten packets would occupy.

`kPeriod` was never clamped against the live queue's actual size anywhere
in this function. On every board this code has been exercised against, the
HW queue is far bigger than 256 packets (`HSA_AGENT_INFO_QUEUE_MAX_SIZE` on
this same gfx803 system reports 131072, matching normal boards), so the
assumption silently held everywhere -- until gfx803, whose
`hsa_queue_create` fails for every requested size above the hard floor of
64 packets (`Device::acquireQueue`'s halving retry loop in rocdevice.cpp
gives up at `queue_size < 64`), for reasons not yet root-caused at the
kernel/KFD level (confirmed via direct `hsa_queue_create` probing and
strace: the ioctl reaches the kernel and returns EINVAL from somewhere
past `set_queue_properties_from_user`'s validation, silently, not from a
struct-ABI mismatch or the power-of-2/minimum-size checks -- both
confirmed fine). A single graph-replay batch of 337 packets against a
64-slot ring (`capacity=63`) reproduced this deterministically, matching
the original diagnostic's `write_index=3257` exactly.

Restoring ROCm 6.4.4's GFXIP7/8 ring-buffer "queue_full_workaround_"
double-mapping mechanism was considered and ruled out as a fix for this:
that mechanism *halves* the theoretical max ring size when active
(`ComputeRingBufferMaxPkts`), it exists to let the CP address a write
index that wraps past one full queue length without losing track via the
doorbell, not to give the ring more usable capacity -- and it is further
gated to `HSA_PROFILE_FULL` (APU) agents only in the original 6.4.4 source,
which a discrete card (`HSA_PROFILE_BASE`) never qualifies for regardless.
It would not have changed this queue's size.

Not gfx803-specific in mechanism -- the fix is a generic dynamic clamp
against the queue's own live capacity (`gpu_queue_->size`, already computed
in this function as `sw_queue_size`), not an ISA/arch gate. It is a no-op
on every board whose queue ends up bigger than `DEBUG_HIP_GRAPH_BATCH_SIZE`
packets, which today is every board except gfx803.

WHAT
----
- rocvirtual.cpp: clamp `kPeriod` to `min(DEBUG_HIP_GRAPH_BATCH_SIZE,
  sw_queue_size)` in `dispatchAqlPacketBatchFlat`.
Applies to: CLR @ rocm-systems release/therock-10.0 (commit 6b0e43f341,
2026-08-29). Re-diffed from the 7.14-era version: dispatchAqlPacketBatchFlat
moved to ~1624, its packet-buffer parameter type changed
(std::vector<uint8_t> -> amd::AlignedVector64<uint8_t>), and upstream added
a ramp-up/lead-chunk path below the kPeriod line that the old hunk's
post-context couldn't match. The unclamped kPeriod = DEBUG_HIP_GRAPH_BATCH_SIZE
is still present and the gfx803 deadlock mechanism is unchanged -- same fix,
new hunk. Verified on real gfx803 hardware on the 7.14 line (see WHY); not
yet re-verified on a 10.0 image.

